Since you are launching from Android Studio's Device Manager (visible in your previous screenshot), Android Studio automatically passes serial console flags to the emulator by default on many Linux setups.
You can easily override this without touching the terminal by creating a single configuration file or adjusting your AVD settings.

1. Disable the Serial Console via advancedFeatures.ini (Permanent Fix)

Because Android Studio hides the serial console toggle in the UI, creating a simple configuration file tells the emulator to turn it off every time you click Play in Device Manager.
• Open your Linux Terminal: Click the >_ icon on your left dock.
• Run this command: Paste the following line and press Enter to disable the serial console feature globally for your emulators:bash
echo "SerialConsole = off" >> ~/.android/advancedFeatures.ini
Use code with caution.
• Verify it was added: Run cat ~/.android/advancedFeatures.ini to make sure SerialConsole = off is listed on its own line.
• (Note: If the notification still appears on certain system images because it is baked into the read-only bootloader, you can safely ignore it—its real-world slowdown is minor compared to graphics and RAM settings).

2. Optimize Your Pixel 5 Hardware Profile in Device Manager

Right now, while you are on the Device Manager screen before booting up, lock in the fastest hardware settings:
• Open Edit Menu: Click the three vertical dots (⋮) on the far right of the Pixel 5 row and select Edit.
• Show Advanced Settings: Scroll down and click Show Advanced Settings.
• Set Graphics: Ensure Graphics is set to Hardware - GLES 2.0 (or Automatic).
• Balance RAM & CPU: Set RAM to 2048 MB (or 3072 MB if your laptop has 8 GB+ of RAM) and Multi-Core CPU to 4 cores so Burp Suite and the emulator run smoothly together.
• Save Changes: Click Finish.

3. Cold Boot Once to Apply Everything

• Launch cleanly: Click the three vertical dots (⋮) next to Pixel 5 again and select Cold Boot Now so Android starts fresh with your updated settings instead of loading an old sluggish snapshot.
